<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alsace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alsace is a historic region of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> mountains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, with Strasbourg as its main city. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> for centuries, it passed largely to France between 1648 and 1681, was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1871 as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> and returned to France in 1918, apart from a further German annexation from 1940 to 1944. Its Alemannic dialect, wines and half-timbered towns reflect this mixed heritage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alsace.jpg" alt="Alsace" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Franganillo from Barcelona, Spain · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colmar_(32350846618).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
